---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro'
import Shot from '../../components/Shot.astro'
import Clip from '../../components/Clip.astro'
---

<Case
  title="Lyrigen + Lyric Studio"
  kicker="Desktop app"
  description="Case study: Lyrigen, a local-first Windows music player with word- and syllable-synced lyrics, beat-matched DJ transitions and on-device AI lyric alignment."
  lede="A free music player for the songs already on your computer. Lyrics follow the voice word by word, songs flow into each other like a DJ set, and nothing you play ever leaves the machine. Lyric Studio, its companion app, times any song's lyrics with AI that runs locally."
  facts={[
    ['Role', 'Solo: design, code, testing, releases'],
    ['Timeline', '2026 – ongoing'],
    ['Stack', 'Electron, React, TypeScript, Vite, Web Audio API'],
    ['AI engine', 'Python, faster-whisper, Meta MMS aligner'],
  ]}
  links={[
    { href: 'https://github.com/mufaddalhirani/lyrigen', label: 'Code on GitHub' },
    { href: 'https://github.com/mufaddalhirani/lyrigen/releases/latest', label: 'Download for Windows' },
    { href: 'https://github.com/mufaddalhirani/lyrics-studio', label: 'Lyric Studio' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="wrap media">
    <Clip
      name="lyrigen-lyrics-1"
      label="Screen recording of Lyrigen playing a song. The current lyric line is highlighted word by word over a moving background made from the album art."
      caption="Word-synced lyrics in the player. The background is generated from the album cover. Muted recording."
    />
  </div>

  <section class="chapter wrap">
    <div class="chapter-grid">
      <h2 class="h2">Why I built it</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I keep a large local music library, and I play guitar and produce music. Streaming apps have great
          synced lyrics but only for their own catalogue. Local players have your files but treat lyrics as an
          afterthought. I wanted both: <strong>my own files, with lyrics as good as the best streaming apps</strong>,
          plus the smooth, beat-matched transitions a DJ would make between songs.
        </p>
        <p>
          The constraints were clear from the start: it had to run on an ordinary Windows PC, open a library of
          thousands of songs instantly, and work offline, with no account and no data leaving the computer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap">
    <div class="chapter-grid">
      <h2 class="h2">How it fits together</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Lyrigen is an Electron app. The <strong>main process</strong> (Node.js) owns the file system:
          scanning the library, reading and writing tags, fetching lyrics from five free sources, and running
          downloads. The <strong>renderer</strong> is a React and TypeScript UI with its own audio engine built on
          the Web Audio API. AI lyric timing runs in a separate <strong>Python process</strong>, so a long job never
          blocks the interface.
        </p>
        <pre class="diagram" role="img" aria-label="Architecture: the React renderer with its Web Audio engine talks to the Electron main process over a preload bridge. The main process reads the music library, fetches lyrics from online sources, and starts Lyric Studio as a Python child process that streams JSON progress events back.">┌──────────────── Renderer (React + TypeScript) ────────────────┐
│  Player · Library · DJ · Lyrics Finder · Sound Lab            │
│  Web Audio: beat analysis · DJ mix bus · EQ · WSOLA worklet   │
└───────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┘
                            │ typed IPC (preload bridge)
┌───────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────┐
│  Electron main process (Node.js)                              │
│  library scan · tags (ID3/Vorbis) · lyric sources · downloads │
└──────────┬───────────────────────────────────┬────────────────┘
           │ files                             │ child process, JSON lines
   ┌───────┴────────┐               ┌──────────┴───────────────┐
   │ Music library  │               │ Lyric Studio (Python)    │
   │ + .ttml / .lrc │               │ faster-whisper, MMS      │
   └────────────────┘               └──────────────────────────┘</pre>
        <p>
          Lyric Studio also ships on its own, with a desktop GUI and a command line. Both apps write the same
          TTML and LRC files, so a song synced in one plays word by word in the other.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap media pair">
    <Shot name="lyrigen-home" alt="Lyrigen home screen: the last-played song set large in a serif typeface, library statistics (5,295 songs, 3,029 albums, 318 hours), and a ‘Jump back in’ list." height={897} caption="Home, on my own library of 5,295 songs." sizes="(min-width: 760px) 520px, 100vw" />
    <Shot name="lyrigen-dj" alt="The DJ screen with two decks, waveforms, BPM readouts, a mixer, the library and an eight-pad sampler." height={900} caption="DJ mode: two decks that find their own tempo and beat grid." sizes="(min-width: 760px) 520px, 100vw" />
  </div>

  <section class="chapter wrap">
    <div class="chapter-grid">
      <h2 class="h2">Four hard problems</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <div class="problem">
          <h3 class="h3">1. Finding the beat, fast and without libraries</h3>
          <p>
            Beat-matched transitions and beat-reactive visuals need each song's exact tempo and the position of
            its first beat. Lyrigen decodes the file once at 11&nbsp;kHz (a quarter of CD rate, enough for kick
            drums), builds an onset curve at 100 frames a second from bass and full-band energy, and finds the
            repeating period with <strong>autocorrelation</strong> between 70 and 180&nbsp;BPM. The search leans towards
            the 90–140&nbsp;BPM range, so a 120&nbsp;BPM song isn't reported as 60 or 240. A <strong>comb search</strong>
            in 0.02&nbsp;BPM steps across every phase then finds the grid whose beats land on the most onsets. That
            gives the precise tempo and the first beat in well under a second. The result is cached per song.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem">
          <h3 class="h3">2. Mixing on the beat without decoding whole songs</h3>
          <p>
            Decoding every song into memory would give sample-exact starts, but costs about 110&nbsp;MB per
            five-minute song. Instead, songs keep streaming from ordinary media elements, and precision comes from
            two places. Every effect (EQ kills, filter sweeps, echo, reverb) is scheduled on the
            <code>AudioContext</code> clock, so it lands exactly on the beat grid. The incoming song starts a moment
            early and silent, and a <strong>phase-locked loop</strong> pulls it onto the outgoing song's beat: one jump
            while silent, then playback-rate nudges of at most 3%. Tempos are matched with pitch-preserving
            time-stretch within ±8%. Beyond that, Lyrigen picks a transition that doesn't need beatmatching.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem">
          <h3 class="h3">3. Changing pitch without changing tempo</h3>
          <p>
            The browser can only change pitch and tempo together. The genre modes needed them separate (lo-fi
            wants a large tempo drop with a smaller pitch drop, so vocals stay clear), so I wrote a
            <strong>WSOLA pitch shifter</strong> as an <code>AudioWorklet</code>: 1,024-sample Hann-windowed grains at 50%
            overlap, with a cross-correlation search that picks the splice point whose waveform best matches
            what was already written, so there are no clicks. Ratios within a cent of 1.0 bypass it entirely.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem">
          <h3 class="h3">4. Lyrics that are timed well, from anywhere</h3>
          <p>
            Lyrigen asks five free lyric sources behind one candidate model and ranks results by how finely they
            are timed: syllable, then word, then line, then plain text. When no good timing exists, Lyric Studio
            makes it. It aligns the known words to the recording with faster-whisper and Meta's MMS forced
            aligner (1,130 languages). It keeps any timing a person already made, checks it against this
            particular recording, and only adds syllables. Syllables are split by spoken syllable in English,
            by akshara in Hindi and other Indic scripts, and by mora in Japanese. <strong>The AI decides timing,
            never spelling</strong>: the output always contains the exact lyrics.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap media pair">
    <Shot name="lyrigen-ai-sync" alt="The ‘Sync with AI’ panel aligning ABBA's ‘The Winner Takes It All’ at syllable level with the large-v3 model on the GPU, keeping the human-made word timing for 61 lines." height={897} caption="AI sync keeps human-made timing for all 61 lines and only adds syllables." sizes="(min-width: 760px) 520px, 100vw" />
    <Shot name="lyrigen-lyrics-finder" alt="Lyrics Finder statistics: 5,282 tracks, 4,169 with lyrics, 685 word-synced, 1,113 missing, above the AI sync form." height={900} caption="Lyrics Finder shows what's missing and fills it." sizes="(min-width: 760px) 520px, 100vw" />
  </div>

  <section class="chapter wrap">
    <div class="chapter-grid">
      <h2 class="h2">Engineering practice</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Typed end to end</strong>: TypeScript in the UI and the Electron main process, with a typed bridge between them.</li>
          <li><strong>Automated checks</strong> driven by Playwright: startup, layout, DJ, lyric-gap and visual-mode smoke tests, plus scripted screenshots for the README.</li>
          <li><strong>Releases</strong>: versioning and release scripts, with an NSIS installer and a single-file portable build from electron-builder.</li>
          <li><strong>Privacy by design</strong>: no account, no telemetry. Lyric lookups send only the song's title, artist or video ID, and the AI runs locally (on the GPU when there is one).</li>
          <li><strong>Performance</strong>: the library opens instantly with thousands of songs, and beat analysis takes under a second per song.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap">
    <div class="chapter-grid">
      <h2 class="h2">What's next</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li>Unit tests for the beat analysis, tempo matching and TTML/LRC parsing, running in GitHub Actions on every push.</li>
          <li>Automated Windows builds in CI and code signing, so Windows stops warning on first launch.</li>
          <li>A small server where people can share lyrics they synced with AI, so nobody has to sync the same song twice.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted">
          What I learned: the hard part of audio software isn't the maths, it's timing. I learned to separate
          what has to be sample-exact from what only has to sound right, and to build for the second without giving up the first.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Case>
